J> I've got RedHat Linux 4.0 installed on my Alpha 150 Jensen system [...]

J> Also, and I think this may be why I'm having problems with the make command,
J> I don't think I properly installed the kernel to begin with.  I followed the
J> doc I found at ftp://ftp.digital.com/pub/DEC/Linux-
J> Alpha/JENSEN_INSTALL/Jensen-HOWTO to install the system.  I was able to
J> follow the doc to a 'T' until I got to the end, and had to actually boot
J> into the install program.  It tells me to type the following command to
J> install "BOOT -FL INSTALL DKA400"  That wouldn't work, I actually had to
J> type 'boot -fi "/kernels/jensen.gz" -fl "root=/dev/scd0" dka400'.  After the
J> install, (everything seems fine), it says to type the following to boot:
J> 'boot -fl "root=0802 ro" dka000'.  This doesn't do anything, so I boot with
J> this command:  'boot dka400 -fi "/kernels/jensen.gz" -fl "root=0802"'  Can
J> someone help me get this system to bootup from the hard disk?  Thanks.

Did you install aboot? Did you write a kernel to the beginning of the disk or
in / or in /boot? If you have installed aboot, you've got an /etc/aboot.conf
file, where you find numbered lines with boot options. Pick the one with your
desired kernel (look if it's really there) and type at the SRM prompt:
boot dka0 -fl "i"

for interactive choice or automatic:

set bootdef_dev dka000
set bootos_flags #
set auto_action boot

where # is the number of your abboot.conf selection.

Viele Gruesse, Christoph




