Peter Doering wrote:
> 
> Hi Folks,
> 
> A few months ago I startet getting problems with the EGCS/GCC compiler
> which comes with the Redhat 5.1 Alpha AXP package. It seems to be quite
> buggy and a compilation run of my favorite 3D-rendering software was not
> successfull, so I got myself the newest EGCS version 1.1.1.
> 
> The problem is that the GCC version which comes with RH 5.1 is needed to
> compile the kernel sources as they contain code which is programmed to
> go around those bugs but does not make sense to the new EGCS compiler
> and a compilation of the kernel fails. However, a lot of software (e.g.
> the Mesa 3 library which I want to compile using the new EGCS) contains
> huge config scripts which rely on the compiler files beeing in its usual
> place and not in /usr/local/bin, where I installed the new EGCS.
> 
> Does someone have an idea how to solve that problem and install
> different C or C ++ compilers on one machine in a way that you can
> compile the kernel sources and software which needs a newer/other
> compiler ?
> 
> Thanks in advance,
> 
> Peter

Hi,

This may not be to everones taste, but this is how I get around the
problem:

Make egcs like this:

$ mkdir /tmp/build
$ cd /tmp/build
$ /usr/local/src/egcs-1.1.2/configure --prefix=/usr/local/egcs-1.1.2
$ make bootstrap

(wait for 40 minutes or so)

$ su
# umask 022
# make install
# cd /usr/local
# ln -s egcs-1.1.2 egcs
# cd /usr/bin
# ln -s /usr/local/egcs/bin/gcc egcc
# ln -s /usr/local/egcs/bin/g++ eg++
# ln -s /usr/local/egcs/bin/g77 eg77

Now anything I want to compile with stock gcc/g++/g77 will use the
default gcc in /usr/bin, while anything I want to use the new egcs
compilers I either change the Makefile to use $(GCC)=egcc, or use
'GCC=egcc; make' instead. This is because the new egcs binaries are NOT
in $PATH of themselves.

Also, if I upgrade egcs I will make into a new directory in /usr/local,
and then change just one link to make the new one current (or revert to
a previous one).

HTH

Best Regards,
Roger.



