hello,

About a week ago, I built my very first Alpha.  I'd never before seen
one in person, let alone installed anything on 'em.  Totally
inexperienced with this architecture, I plowed in.

Six and a half hours and a bald spot later, I had NT Server 4.0
installed.  Another half hour and a relaxing cigarette later, Red Hat
5.2 was comfortable installed.  NT took ~10 reboots to run.  Linux, just
one--the one after the installation!

On the x86 side of the fence, I just finished putting Windoze 98 on my
mom's AMD K6-200.  It rebooted itself 8 times.  Granted, I didn't have
to tell it much of anything except an inordinately huge registration
number, but it's slow and crashes itself just for the kick of it. 
Slackware Linux, on the other hand, installs without rebooting at all,
and has uptimes in years, not picoseconds.

Seems to me that the important part isn't how pretty your OS is, rather
how robust and streamlined.

Just my opinion.........

Dan

