>> When will there be a truly GUI based version of Linux
>
>In my opinion, it is in the process of happening.  There is not one,
>but two competing desktop environments for Linux/UNIX under development.

There's a "distinct" point of view which goes something like...

"Linux is an operating system kernel, which means that ``truly
GUI-based'' infers that X and other stuff is being folded in with the
kernel."

That is the architecture that Microsoft has taken as they have moved to
NT and its derivatives, and is something that is definitely *NOT* going
to happen with Linux. 

>I'm partial to gnome (www.gnome.org); there is also KDE (www.kde.org).
>
>These are in the process of shipping as part of the next major versions
>of Linux distributions (RH 6.0, SUSE 6.1, etc, Caldera, etc.).  Certainly
>by the end of the summer Linux will look very different than it has in
>the past.

There is a common misconception that these projects represent "window
managers," which is ludicrously incorrect. 

GNOME and KDE represent two things:

a) Application frameworks that include libraries that provide a variety
of "application services."

b) Sets of applications written to those frameworks. 

From the developer's Point Of View, the important thing is the
framework. 

Most of the publicity has had to do with the sets of applications.

In the long run, the framework is the important thing. 

X applications have tended to need to recreate, typically from scratch,
such things as:
- Configuration file formats/readers
- Interapplication communications
- Interfacing with window managers
- Database access
- Document parsing
- Documentation access [e.g. - "Help Files"]
...

GNOME and KDE provide libraries to provide some common ways of getting
at these sorts of things, which allows application developers to spend
their time building real functionality rather than having to recreate
this stuff over and over... 

>> I was at a Birthday party over the weekend - a bunch of University Users
>> that swear by Microsoft were arguing that maybe if Linux was as easy to use
>> and install as NT, then they would perhaps try it out
>
>To be fair, NT isn't all that easy to install from scratch.  Linux isn't
>much more difficult than that, if your hardware is supported.

And both have "hardware compatibility lists" that are finite. 

>> I can't believe that something like Motif hasn't been adapted, or adopted,
>> to be integrated in Linux
>
>Motif / CDE is royalty bearing, and at a price that the general Linux 
>market is impossible.  It is available as a layered product. I've never 
>been fond if them anyway, and have never thought they were serious 
>competition to the Mac or Windows.  

General deployment of CDE (implying Motif) on Linux *looks* like it
costs not far from $150 for Open Group licensing fees.  Take the
on-the-order-of-ten million users of Linux, and that implies TOG
"sucking up" $1.5B in licensing fees.  Not a likely scenario.

It's almost certainly worth a few million dollars to have GNOME/KDE be
"relatively free."  The wars over KDE and Qt have occurred as a result
of arguing that Qt isn't "free enough..."

>Gnome (at least; haven't played with 
>kde as much) certainly is... I get a charge out of seeing the X window 
>System work the way we intended... 

I think a fair quote of this, for "cookie" purposes is:

"Gnome certainly is (serious competition to the Mac or Windows) ... I
get a charge out of seeing the X Window System work the way we
intended..." - Jim Gettys
