Hello,
	I run an old alpha multia.  I have linux on it and I have had NT &
DEC Unix 4.0D on it.  For what you want to do an alpha would & could do
it.  You would run the ALPHABios or ARC, ARC will let you decide which OS
you want to boot.  There is a small system partition in the front of your
drive (usually 1-3mgs) which will have your boot loaders.  For NT - it
will take care of its self - for linux it will put milo and linload into
that partition.  It works very well & in my opinion efficently.  I think
rather than going out and buying a new alpha right off the bat, you may
want to find an old multia or decstation.  I reccomend around 233-350 mhz
you can find them from $100-$350 depending on the options you need.  It
will give you an idea of how the bios works together with the os.  The are
many differences between that an Intel ver of Linux & NT in regards to how
the bios handle it.  I'm not sure if that helps of confuses, I hope it
helps.

Anthony

