Larry Kilgallen wrote:
> I, on the other hand, feel the removal of technology as a basis for
> competition would lead to competition based solely on color, price,
> brand recognition, and other factors quite divorced from quality.

You mean just exactly as it is today?

I also don't buy the leading by compiler technology argument you brought
in the previous post. Yes, I'm aware that the Alpha compiler team
managed to tune one of the SPECint benchmarks by a ridiculous factor. If
I remember right, soon after that the Intel compiler specialists tuned
the same benchmark by a yet more ridiculous factor. The compiler
specialists at Motorola didn't succeed to tune their compiler to the
same amount as Intel's, and found out that Intel's specialists had their
part overtuned, and it produced wrong results that haven't been catched
by the SPECint suite.

The lesson we learn is that reverse engineering already happens. The
exchange of technology seems to work even without open source. It's just
more expensive, and after all, noone benefits from that but the big
companies (thus a big company can affort to reverse engineer the
competitor's product, whereas the small companies just can hope to
develop superiour technology first - and then be ripped of by the
reverse engineerers).

BTW: the point about open source is - despite of product splits and ego
plays we sometimes see - not to compete, but to cooperate. If DEC's
compiler specialists don't want to share their knowledge, we'll end up
using the code Intel's and HP's specialists put into the Merced GCC
port. This will give us a fast Alpha GCC, but perhaps later than DEC
wants.

