> It was something like (but this doesn't work)...by the way,
> this is from the SRM console and I didn't need any floppies.
> 
> >>>boot dka400 -file kernels/xl.gz -flags load_ramdisk=1
> root=/dev/sr0

Almost there!  You need quotation marks to quote all flags together. 
According to Red hat, you don't seem to need load_ramdisk; also root
device would be /dev/scd0 (sr0 is for NFS).  Since you are using AS200,
I suspect you should be using avanti-s.gz instead of xl.gz.

Anyhow, this is only theory.  On my AS200, I could never boot this way. 
It always told me that root file system cannot be mounted.  I had to
play a nasty trick, to use dka400 as boot device, then use load_ramdisk
AND /dev/fd0 as root file system:-(  That's it, using the CD-ROM to boot
because the boot diskette I produced from Red Hat CD-ROM didn't work,
but using the RAM disk on diskette as root because the CD-ROM is not
mountable.

Hope this helps. (Also could you please E-mail me when you succeed
without resorting to floppy?  My news server sucks.)

