Paul DeMone (pdemone@igs.net) wrote:
:    I guess I was unclear.  I meant that a binary recompiler could take
:    the Alpha executable image generated by gcc and recompile and reschedule
:    the code.

The compiler algorithms may depend on information that is in the source
code but not in the assembly (or gcc intermediate representation). This
whole model of a compiler as a language dependent front-end talking to a
machine and language independent optimizer talking to a machine dependent
back-end is highly idealized and not all compiler (even gcc) work that way.

Now I suppose you can say all information is in the binary. And I suppose
that's true in some sense if you have access to the entirety of the bits
that are used in the binary and an infinite amount of time to do the
optimization :-)

:    BTW, if you think gcc internals are obsolete for good Alpha code
:    generation, wait until someone tries to graft on EPIC compiler
:    technology.  The compiler gurus unequivocally say that to compile
:    properly for EPIC you essentially have to start from scratch.

Well, the EPIC compiler gurus are pretty heavily invested in certain
compiler technologies... Fortunately, some of that technology (Trimaran) is
