Those things, you realize, are about equivalent to a Pentium 133MHz?  (Or
even slower).  I wouldn't want such a thing simply because it'd use
valuable space....

>    However, I still haven't had this answered: from a general point of
>view, how do Alpha's at 533MHz compare with say, a PIII at 500 or
>550MHz. In terms of speed. And is the architecture more "powerful" than

I'd rather have a PIII/500, frankly.  Software support is better,
it's signifcantly faster on integer stuff than a 21164A/533, 
(20.4 vs 16.7 SPECint95) though still signficantly slower than a 21164
on FP: (14.7 vs 19.5 SPECfp95).

>that of an X86 chip? I know the architecture is RISC and has fewer
>instructions but how does the Alpha's architecture and capability
>compare against an X86?

Well, let me put it this way:  the individual instructions are simpler,
but the Alpha instruction  arch includes more registers.  I'd much
rather program in alpha assembly than x86.

>    Also, how do the SPECint95 benchmarks work?

They run a bunch of different integer-oriented programs and take the
geometric mean.  If you want more detail, look at www.specbench.org.

PeterM




