In article <7g0cpc$53e$1@nnrp1.dejanews.com>, david_morse@my-dejanews.com writes:
|> Hi,
|> Does anyone know where I can find the calling conventions for linux on alpha?

They are the same as for OSF/1 (or Digital Unix or TrueUnix or what it is called
today). If found a document called 'OSF/1 calling standard' or the like at the
Digital Website some time ago.

In short: 
$0/$f0: return value
$1-$8: scratch, not saved
$9-$14: scratch, must be saved by callee
$15(fp): frame pointer
$16-$21/$f16-$f21: procedure parameters (remaining on stack), can be modified
$22-$25: scratch, not saved
$26(ra): return address
$27(pv): procedure value (ie. start address of called code)
$29(gp): global pointer
$30(sp): stack pointer

Some of the FP-regs also have to be preserved during a call...
-- 
