In comp.arch Dave Cherkus <cherkus@unimaster.com> wrote:
> Erik Corry (erik@arbat.com) wrote:
>> 
>> Why 'unfortunately'?  Has this feature limited gcc's portability?

> It surely makes it hard to compile code written using this construct
> with compilers other than gcc.

If you care you can make gcc issue warnings/errors for this sort
of thing.

> I've been told that the only compiler
> that can compile the Linux kernel code is gcc/egcs.  It'd be nice to
> see if the upcoming Compaq Alpha compilers for Linux produce a faster
> kernel, but I've been told the work involved in eliminating/recoding
> gcc constructs is huge.

Does the Compaq compiler allow you to embed gas assembler
snippets in your code?  That is a very valid thing for a kernel
to want to do, and the Linux kernel does it all over the place.
Until that is possible, minor things like label variables are
not worth thinking about.

The Linux kernel (in eg /usr/src/include/asm-i386/uaccess.h) also
does some clever stuff, setting up a sort of simple exception
handler that handles programs passing bad pointers to system
calls with 0 overhead in the common case.  Not even the icache
is hit with the error handling code, since it is put in a different
section.  This is one of the reasons Linux does so well in lmbench
scores (when's the next version due out, Larry?).

So, yes, the Linux kernel uses a lot of gcc extensions, but there
are good reasons for it most of the time.

