Why did you eliminate first-class labels, and why do you find their
inclusion in gcc unfortunate?

My experiences with gcc's labels-as-values feature is very positive.
It provides a significant speedup for the interpreter I work on, and I
don't see any disadvantages.

In numbers: the speedup factor of Gforth (which uses threaded code
with labels-as-values) over PFE (which uses an ANSI C compliant
technique which I call "call threading") on a number of benchmarks is
of 1.5-1.74 on a 486/66.

Followups to comp.lang.c.

