Sorry if Ive missed something, I started following this discussion late in the
game, but having looked at http://www.compaq.com/newsroom/pr/1999/pr060499a.html
Im not sure that the current price point for this compiler ($2500 and up) is 
going to help Compaq's bottom line or many Linux users.

It looks like the same pricing strategy as for video tapes,- you can decide that
the only person that will by the tape is a video store, and they are going to 
make lots of money renting it, so you charg $89.95 for the tape, OR you can
go for the end user and sell them for $19.95 (or less), sell a lot more, and
make as much or more money.

At $2500 a crack, this is definately the first strategy.

On my speed tests of a Pentium II/350MHz vs an Alpha 164, I got essentially 
equal speed for a number of different tests with g77.

The press release says that Digital Fortran is x2 as fast as g77.

Fine, but at todays prices, I could easily buy one or two more Pentium's, 
probably 400MHz, for the $2500 it would cost me to upgrade my compiler on
the Alpha.

If all I care about is getting my calculations done faster, and am not interested
in an Intel/Dec/Compaq religious war, whats the percentage of spending $2500
for a compiler some time later this year, when hardware prices will be cheaper
yet (and g77 might even be a bit smarter)?????

						Reg.Clemens
						reg@dwf.com



