There are many Alpha OEM vendors out there, have a look at
http://www.alpha-processor.com/where-to-buy/vendor_systems.asp for a
comprehensive list.

>     But one question that has come up when I look through documentation
> on Compaq and Digital's sites is this: how does an Alpha system's BIOS
> work.
>
> I have heard of 2 BIOS options: something called AlphaBIOS which NT
> apparently requires and something along the lines of SRM for DEC UNIX
> (Tru64 UNIX now). Can anyone clear this up for me? What O/S options am I
> limited too? I'd be looking into NT/Linux dual boot.

For UNIX and VMS, SRM console is required. Some boards do not have SRM
capabilities. If you wish to run a dual boot Linux / NT box without any plans
for UNIX or VMS, then you do not need to concern yourself with this issue. All
current Alpha systems will boot NT and Linux.

>     Although this isn't for work I thought it would be nice (once my
> Linux skills solidify on X86) to try out an Alpha for development
> purposes. I am aware of the commercial software limitations. Is there
> any way to lease an Alpha system? Is it worth looking into?

There are not many commercial software packages available for Linux on any
hardware period, this is not an Alpha / Linux issue. This is changing. Many
Alpha vendors have lease options, check around.

It is only worth looking into if you plan on buying / leasing one soon... 3
or 4 years from now, I would suspect that this would be a waste of time, but
it is your time obviously.


