In article <371A0B6C.4E678E8@gmx.de>,
 Bernd Paysan <bernd.paysan@gmx.de> writes:
> The other effect is also understandable:
> it's just like the Pentium doesn't like back-to-back branches, and when
> you move the jump into the next chunk, it's not a back-to-back branch.

Could be.  I have done some more measurements: this time I aligned the
jump target, and had the load (+add) and the jump in various 16-byte
groups, and filled the rest of the instructions with nops and
unops.

I.e.

$L2:
{nop;unop;nop;unop}
ldq $1,0($2)
addq $2,8,$2
[nop;nop]
{nop;unop;nop;unop}
jmp $31,($1),0

This resulted in:

jump		load group
group	0	1	2	3
0	1.12s
1	0.72s	2.64s
2	1.16s	2.68s	2.84s
3	1.12s	2.64s	2.82s	3.00s
4	1.28s	1.52s	2.82s	3.01s
5	1.44s	1.70s	1.70s	3.01s
6	1.62s	1.90s	1.89s	1.89s

If the load is not in group 0, the pattern seems to be: if the jump
happens before the loaded value is available (the load latency is 3
cycles), it mispredicts and does not update the next-line predictor of
the jump.  For some reason it works a lot better with the load in
group 0.

I am a bit worried, because many of these patterns occur in a real
interpreter.  However, I don't know how the presence of instructions
other than nops and unops affects these results (but my experiences
until now indicate that they do), so I probably should stop worrying
and wait until the optimization guide arrives.

- anton
