In article <7fktov$a95$1@nnrp1.dejanews.com>, marc_dubrowski@kbinirsnb.be wrote:
> Hi,
> I have to setup both a mail server and a DNS server on a AS200 4/166
> This Workstation has 1 GB SCSI Hard drive, and 32 MB of RAM.
> 
> The Linux version comes from the redhat 5.2 distribution, with the 2.0.35
> kernel.
> 
> We here have 250 users to provide with these services.
> 
> The question is: do I need to upgrade , and (as I presume the answer will be
> yes) how?

You have plenty of processor to provide mail and DNS service.  You
will most likely want to upgrade your RAM to at least 64 MB, or more
likely 128 MB.  Depending on how much mail you handle and what kind of
mail, you may also need more disk space, perhaps 2-9 GB.  A bare
install (kernel, compilers, BIND, sendmail, POP, no X) should take on
the order of 200 MB or less, so you'll have to gauge your usage to
find out if 1GB is enough for you.

As far as memory goes:  you will have to run tests to find out how
much memory each POP session takes on the server.  I'm guessing that
it will be on the order of 500k-1MB.  Your DNS service will probably
consume a dozen megs or so.  You're not going to be serving many POP
sessions with 32 MB...

My personal preference for vendors are MemoryX (www.memoryx.com) and
Dirt Cheap Drives (www.dirtcheapdrives.com).  You'll want to look in
the documentation for your AlphaStation and in the FAQ files on
www.alphalinux.org to find out exactly what kind of memory you need.

Being in Belgium (I think) you'll most likely want to find a local
source for those items.

Good luck.

-andy



