Dave Cherkus wrote:
> 
> Erik Corry (erik@arbat.com) wrote:
> >
> > Why 'unfortunately'?  Has this feature limited gcc's portability?
> >
> 
> It surely makes it hard to compile code written using this construct
> with compilers other than gcc.  I've been told that the only compiler
> that can compile the Linux kernel code is gcc/egcs.  It'd be nice to
> see if the upcoming Compaq Alpha compilers for Linux produce a faster
> kernel, but I've been told the work involved in eliminating/recoding
> gcc constructs is huge.

But adding this GCC feature to any other C compiler should be pretty
trivial, what? This is the "don't fix your app, fix your compiler"
approach. GCC has so many valuable extensions that are needed to write
efficient code, and labels as values is just one of them - Linux uses
others. So maybe recoding Linux might lead to compilable code on
DECpaq's compiler, but it doesn't necessarily mean that this code will
run faster.

Well, IMHO the whole thing is all the wrong approach. The Alpha compiler
team should better improve GCC instead of trying to port Linux over to a
non-GCC compiler.

But back to the topic: The effect of the instructions in the 16 byte
chunk before the label target is easy to explain: obviously the 21264
just executes all of them, and cancels those that shouldn't have been
executed - that takes time. The other effect is also understandable:
it's just like the Pentium doesn't like back-to-back branches, and when
you move the jump into the next chunk, it's not a back-to-back branch.

GCC has some simple hooks to define alignments of branch targets, the
pgcc makes use of that, too; setting them to 16 bytes (and padding with
nops) should be easy. I don't know if it also allows padding of jumps,
but after all emiting a .align per jump should be not so difficult. With
ELF, these alignments actually work.

-- 
