In article <7fdn5h$e24$1@tesla.EECS.Berkeley.EDU>,
Peter Mardahl <peterm@tesla.EECS.Berkeley.EDU> wrote:
>
>I've read somewhere that gcc's fundamental design (cross-platform, etc)
>and some of the abstraction layers make it very, very hard to do certain
>kinds of optimizations, which DEC CC can do, or in other words,
>that gcc would have to be redesigned to get performance to match
>DEC CC.

  Yes, it needs to be redesigned.  The cross-platform orientation of
the compiler has little to do with it, though.  The intermediate lang-
guage (RTL) is ancient and makes it hard to perform certain modern
optimizations.  Also, not enough optimization is performed at the 
parse-tree level.

  Replacing all of the RTL stuff would mean rewriting most of the 
compiler, something that people accustomed to developing gcc are 
loathe to do, especially since there's a lot of work that can be 
done to improve the compiler without going so far as total rewrite.

>I unfortunately can't cite a source for this, but it makes me
>reluctant to tell DEC (or anyone else) that they should "fix" gcc.

  I don't see DEC's motivation, frankly.  They've already designed
and implemented their own compiler.  If they "should" do anything,
perhaps they should add gcc's C enhancements to their compiler.

  -- TTK




