Bob Koehler <koehler@bessta.gsfc.nasa.aspm.gov> wrote:
>Mean while, if you buy a Fortran compiler from Sun, HP, MS, probably
>others, you will hear a prhase like "VAX Fortran Compatable".  Fortran
>95 may be the ANSI and/or ISO standard, but VAX Fortran is the defacto
>industry standard.
Unfortunately, that's a standard which isn't defined very well.  Many
compilers on the market claim compatibility with VAX FORTRAN, but none
offer all of the extensions available on VMS.  Our company was forced to
create a tool to precompile VMS FORTRAN in order to properly target
compilers which supposedly were "VMS compatible"...and the precompiler
has to do a lot.

Most vendors provide %val and VMS-style structures...many do not provide
the 'Q' FORMAT descriptor, indexed file I/O (even Digital/Compaq doesn't,
saying indexed files are not a language feature), various VMS FORTRAN
intrinsic functions, and so on.  I believe the actual standards implemented
by vendors have more to do with military specs which have come to include
certain VMS extensions.

Most of the commercial offerings we've seen are a lot better with
VMS FORTRAN code than the current g77, though.
