on Mon, 19 Apr 1999 17:26:26 -0500, Vivek Venugopalan  wrote
regarding "How do I configure Networking":

> 
> 
> On Mon, 19 Apr 1999, Dang H. Nguyen wrote:
> 
>> On Mon, 19 Apr 1999 10:04:06 -0500, Vivek Venugopalan
>> <vivek@nothis.mcs.net> wrote:
>> 
>>>Hello
>>>	I finally got my AS200 up and running using MILO and the nice 
>>> instructions that everyone has provided.  Now, I would like to make 
>>> my NT pc talk to my alpha.  I threw in a network card into my NT box 
>>> and set its IP to 192.100.100.2.  On the linux box, I set hte IP to 
>>> 192.100.100.1.  Took a piece of coax and hooked htem up (with 
>>> terminators ofcourse).  Fair enough, the alpha's start up message says 
>>> Cannot find 10BaseT beat, switching to AUI media
>>                            ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
>> That's not coax. I thought coax was BNC? 
>> 
> 
> Yes Coax is BNC... so what is AUI media?  and how do I make it use Coax?

Couple of points.

1. The IP addresses you use are *not* "private". Before you have those
boxes talk to the internet - in any form, shape or colour - change
them. 192.168.*.* is a good start. Note: this is important if you use
Network Address Translation (or masquerading, as Linux calls it). If
you have a "real" connection, get real addresses from your provider.

2. The AUI port is the 15-pin sub-d connector. That requires some sort
of transceiver (a little box) to convert it to whatever media you're
actually using; a 10b2 (cheapernet, what you're trying to use)
transceiver costs around DM 60 (US$ 35). 15-pin male connector one
side, BNC the other.

3. Some alphas have the BNC connector mainly for show, it appears, and
cannot actually make use of it. DEC Unix certainly won't cooperate
with it on the multia, whereas Linux will. Try getting a cross cable
and plugging that into the RJ-45 (10bT) ports, which is good enough
for just two hosts - better still, do the right thing straight away 
and be expandable to multiple boxes on the same network and get a 
mini-hub (around US$ 35, I believe) and a couple of real cat5 cables.


Gabe
