cbbrowne@news.brownes.org (Christopher B. Browne) writes:

> You might, for instance, enlighten us from your apparently "infinite"
> wisdom on the computing industry as to precisely *why* the Compaq
> compiler is "better."

Christopher:

You may not be aware of this.  I have responded to this thread once
before, but I guess it won't hurt to do so again.

There is a whole world of engineering analysts out there who, for
whatever reason, are required to modify and run large FORTRAN codes
for a living.  These codes have been around for years, and will live
for many more years so long as there is a need for them.  Don't even
mention running them in C or C++!  The cost to convert them would be
so great as these codes typically might be 100s or 1000s of lines of
source; i.e., very large code.  Hence, there definitely is a market
for FORTRAN compilers on fast machines.

These same people are also always looking for a less expensive and
faster way of running these codes.  In comes the Alpha.  Great
machine.  However, the Linux g77 compliler lacks the Compaq f77
compiler in several areas.  I am sure the developers of g77 are aware
of these areas as well.  Below are two of these reasons:

(1) the final executable runs significantly faster from the Compaq
    commercial product.  So much faster in fact that it is worth the
    extra expense to get results back quicker.

(2) the g77 compiler on the Alpha does not produce code that will
    respond to floating point exceptions in the same way as it does on
    the Compaq commercial product (f77) nor in the same way as g77
    does on the Intel platform. (see the -fpe1, -fpe2, etc. switches
    available on the Compaq f77 product).

These are well-known facts.  One can test these directly by executing
the final executable files from a Compaq compile on a Linux Alpha.

One of these people who do this and who are very willing to pay Compaq
for a FORTRAN compiler on Linux is myself.  I know of at least one
other person who has told me directly he is also willing to do the
same for his Linux Alpha.  He is a private consultant and does this
type of thing in his business.  So you might say that he will willing
to pay from his own pocket for this.  I am sure Compaq feels there are
many more FORTRAN runners who are willing to pay for a good FORTRAN
compiler for the Alpha running under Linux.

I hope this clears the air a bit on this subject.



