Phillip Helbig wrote:
> 
> In article <87d81hkqw0.fsf@pfaffben.user.msu.edu>, Ben Pfaff <pfaffben@pilot.msu.edu> writes:
> 
> > Compaq can kiss my ass if they think I'm going to be grateful for a
> > Linux port of *anything* that's not open source.
> 
> Compaq is a commercial company.  If you don't like it, don't buy their
> products.
> 
> If they go open source, there will not be enough revenue to support
> development.

That, of course, is open to debate.  I wouldn't be surprised if
something other than simple cost/benefit prohibits them from publishing
the source of the compiler.

Then again:  "Open Source is no magic pixie dust".  If you think you've
got a good, solid, compiler team that produces a great product (at least
*I* think Digital Fortran is a great product) and you don't want them
being sidetracked by a deluge of e-mails from people who can read the
source but do not understand it, it might be a wise decision just to
keep it closed.

> It is of course no coincidence that the commercial Compaq compiler is
> better than a gnu compiler.  This is not a criticism of gnu, just the
> statement of an obvious fact.  (As far as commercial compilers which are
> worse than gnu compilers, that speaks more for the lack of quality of
> those commercial compilers.)

Hmm, I miss some intelligent reasoning here.  So I'll present some
myself:  If the EGCS team gets the same information as the Compaq
compiler team, then in principle it should be possible to produce as
good a compiler.  However, (1) the EGCS team cannot start working on it
earlier than that the information becomes publicly available and (2) if
it requires the representation of features new in the latest chips, it
might take some time until we have generalized that sufficiently to be
able to come up with an improvement to egcs that encompasses this new
feature for several architectures.

As for a full Fortran-95 compiler:  That's simply a lot of work
(probably too much to develop on a volunteer basis).  Note that the
GNAT-95 and C++ Front Ends were both developed by teams being paid for
their work.

> For whatever reasons, some people want to run linux but have a better
> compiler.  If that's what they need, then Compaq supporting their
> compilers for linux is what they've been waiting for.  If it goes
> against your philosophy, don't use it.
> 
> Or does Stallman now say it is immoral to support non-open-source
> software on a gpl platform?!

I've never heard him complain about the plethora of commercial compilers
on Intel/Linux, so that would highly surprise me.

If he would have, he would certainly have frased the GPL differently. 
No version of the GPL ever prohibited someone from using GPL'd and
closed software together - its function is to prohibit the hoarding of
freely available software *into* closed products.

