Anton Ertl wrote:

>
> Here's what egcs-1.03 produces for the first NEXT:
>
> 0x120000690 <main+16>:  ldah    t2,153(t2)
> next:
> 0x120000694 <main+20>:  ldq     t0,0(t1)
> 0x120000698 <main+24>:  addq    t1,0x8,t1
> 0x12000069c <main+28>:  jmp     zero,(t0),0x1200006a0 <main+32>
>
> This takes 3.45s (~17 cycles/NEXT)

The following behavior may be explained by the cache architecture?

Here a short description of the prefetch unit on the 21264, a more detailed
discussion can probably be found in Microprocessor Report (just don't read the
sections which explain why Intel will inevitably win...)

Each line in the instruction cache holds four instructions along with
a "next-line" predictor and a set predictor. These two fields
are immediately sent back to the cache inputs to start the next
access. If the line contains a branch that is predicted taken, the
predictor fields point to the cache line and set of the predicted
target; otherwise, they simply point to the next sequential
address.



>
>
> Here are various variations on this code, with instructions before the
> "next:" jump target in parentheses:
>
> 0x1200006a0    ldq      (nop)   (ldah)   (addq)   nop
> 0x1200006a4    addq     ldq     ldq      ldq      nop
> 0x1200006a8    jmp      addq    addq     addq     ldq
> 0x1200006ac             jmp     jmp      jmp      addq
> 0x1200006b0                                       jmp
>                1.12s    2.56s   3.48s    3.45s    0.72s
>
> The conclusions I draw are:
>
> 1) Code should be aligned to 16-byte boundaries; Penalty for
> non-conformance: ~8 cycles/NEXT in this benchmark. Why so much?

Yoy probably read the whole line, whether you need it or not -- the fetch
probably decodes the whole line also, since the predictor described above does
not actually indicate that you know WHERE in the line you will land...  you
later may have to discard those instructions?

>
>
> 2) In an unaligned block the kind of instruction *before* the jump
> target does play a role.  nops are cheap, while the other instructions
> I tried apparently cost another ~5 cycles/NEXT. Why is that and why so
> much?

Maybe the architecture always decodes everything, even if it would not have to
-- see above... NOPs would be cheaper in this scheme of things.

>
>
> 3) Pushing the jmp into the next 16-byte block by adding nops saves
> ~2cycles/NEXT.  Why?
>

Two guesses for (3)
1 - Some bias for double word aligned lines (one MUX level less), combined with
the cheapness of NOPs?
2 - Maybe the architects tried to be really smart about this, and can get the
branch for free with some trickery, e.g., set the current lines next line
predictor to itself and skip the branch??? This is just a wild guess, since to
actually get something like this correct could be truely painful...

