In article <7ej80q$o1a$1@news.tuwien.ac.at>,
Anton Ertl <anton@mips.complang.tuwien.ac.at> wrote:


>2) In an unaligned block the kind of instruction *before* the jump
>target does play a role.  nops are cheap, while the other instructions
>I tried apparently cost another ~5 cycles/NEXT. Why is that and why so
>much?

Since no one who really knows has seen fit to answer:

The 21264 uses a next-line-and-set predictor.  That is, the predictor
doesn't emit a predicted PC value.  It emits I-cache indexing
information which allows you to cycle the I-cache.

The advantage of this scheme is that 
-the predictor state has fewer bits/target, eg 12 instead of 43.
-you don't have to map PC bits to an I-cache line-and-set, it's already done.

The disadvantage (?) of this is:
-if the I-cache misses, the I-refill doesn't have the speculated PC.

From your data, I'd guess that they're prefetching the branch target's
cache line, and speculatively issuing the left hand of the cache line.
If the target wasn't at the left hand of the cache line, their
mis-speculation has some time cost.  I'd guess that the issue logic
squashes NOPs, although NOPs may be fast-pathed in some other
way.

I don't know why 5 cycles.

The moral is that on the 21264, you will see speedup if you use
binaries with cache-aligned branch targets.  Compaq has tooling to
re-schedule old binaries, but I guess the customer has to know to run
them.
-- 
