You know, theer's a golden rule I'd suggest you follow: check your
facts before you blurt [1].

The web page on g77 (http://www.gnu.org/software/fortran/fortran.html)
contains the following text:

 g77 is a free Fortran 77 compiler.

Granted, you need the gcc source to be able to compile g77.  So what,
it only means that they share the middle engine (the g77 source only
contains the front-end part).  That doesn't make it less a compiler.
If I remember correctly, DEC C and DEC C++ are built in a similar way.

GNU GNAT (an Ada95 compiler) is also made that way.

