> I.e., if we want to write decent compilers for the Alpha, instead of
> reading an optimization guide (which, e.g., Intel and AMD provide for
> free for their CPUs) we have to buy a compiler from Compaq, look at
> the code it generates, and then measure whether the things it does are
> genuine optimizations or artifacts of the optimizer/code generator.
> 
> And no, appendix A of the Alpha AXP Architecture reference manual does
> not cut it. Its "factor of" terminology is unclear, and, e.g., it does
> not explain the results I see on a 21264 when I vary
> http://www.complang.tuwien.ac.at/forth/threading/direct.c.
> 

Why would you expect to find the information you need to
optimize in the *ARCHITECTURE* manual?  There are multiple
implementations of the chip architecture, and then there
are an even larger number of systems they plug into, which
also affects performance.  The architecture spec gives you
what you can rely on across multiple implementations only.

So you need to go look at the specifications of the chips
(and potentially) systems themselves to get the information
to optimize compilers.

You'll find the chip related spec's in: 
http://ftp.digital.com/pub/Digital/info/semiconductor/literature/dsc-library=
.html

And therein you'll find the specs for the various Alpha chips,
which go into gorey detail on the pipelines, instruction issue
conflicts, etc.

At a higher level, you then have to know something about the parameters 
of the systems the Alpha's plug into to get the complete picture (e.g. 
external cache organization and clocking parameters used for a given clock 
rate).  Some of the OEM Alpha board specs are there as well; I dunno if 
the specs for the larger systems are online or not.

In any case, the *architecture* spec is not the place to find the
information you are looking for.

I hope this helps.
				- Jim Gettys



