>>
>> Hmmm, I didn't realize there's actual evidence that the information in
>> the Alpha Architecture Manual is not sufficient
>
>Ok, I wanted to write this for some time, so here we go:
>
>The C program is this:
>
>#define NEXT goto **ip++
>main()
>{
>  static void  *prog[] = {&&next,&&next,&&next,&&next,&&next,
>   &&next,&&next,&&next,&&next,&&loop};
>  void **ip=prog;
>  int    count = 10000000;
>
> next:
>  NEXT;
> loop:
>  if (count>0) {
>    count--;
>    ip=prog;
>    NEXT;
>  }
>}
>
>Here's what egcs-1.03 produces for the first NEXT:
>
>0x120000690 <main+16>:  ldah    t2,153(t2)
>next:
>0x120000694 <main+20>:  ldq     t0,0(t1)
>0x120000698 <main+24>:  addq    t1,0x8,t1
>0x12000069c <main+28>:  jmp     zero,(t0),0x1200006a0 <main+32>
>
>This takes 3.45s (~17 cycles/NEXT)
>
>Here are various variations on this code, with instructions before the
>"next:" jump target in parentheses:
>
>0x1200006a0    ldq      (nop)   (ldah)   (addq)   nop
>0x1200006a4    addq     ldq  ldq      ldq      nop
>0x1200006a8    jmp      addq addq     addq     ldq
>0x1200006ac jmp  jmp      jmp      addq
>0x1200006b0                   jmp
>               1.12s    2.56s   3.48s    3.45s    0.72s
>
>The conclusions I draw are:
>
>1) Code should be aligned to 16-byte boundaries; Penalty for
>non-conformance: ~8 cycles/NEXT in this benchmark. Why so much?
>
>2) In an unaligned block the kind of instruction *before* the jump
>target does play a role.  nops are cheap, while the other instructions
>I tried apparently cost another ~5 cycles/NEXT. Why is that and why so
>much?
>
>3) Pushing the jmp into the next 16-byte block by adding nops saves
>~2cycles/NEXT.  Why?
>
>Overall I see a factor of five variation in speed by simply inserting
>a few nops in the right spots.
>
>Crossposted to comp.os.linux.alpha,comp.arch.
>
>- anton
