On 9 Apr 1999 10:30:04 -0500, cherkus@unimaster.com (Dave Cherkus)
wrote:

>When I used to work on C compilers, the 'acid test' was compiling the
>kernel.  I'm wondering if that is in the plan for your compiler on
>Alpha Linux.  I understand the Linux kernel may have dependencies on
>gcc 'extensions' but I wonder if they can't be worked around.  I
>wouldn't want to see the product delayed for this test, but it may
>provide interesting data.

While the kernel certainly serves as a useful test case for gcc
compatibility, it is not our immediate goal that our C compiler be
able to compile the kernel.  But we certainly consider that desireable
in the long run and see it as an opportunity to improve the kernel's
performance.  Of course, then there would be the issue of how such a
recompiled kernel and the compiler would be distributed.  Lots of open
issues there which we've noted but we're not presently worrying about.
First things first...



