Greg Lindahl wrote in message <7eqss4$n6u@news1.newsguy.com>...

>Is this some kind of bizarre litmus test that we've all missed? g77 is
>a Fortran 77 compiler. It doesn't claim to be anything more. g77
>actually beats Digital Fortran on one of the spec95fp benchmarks last
>time I tested, so it occasionally is pretty good. Praise it for what
>it does well, realize what it doesn't do well, but don't say weird
>things like "g77 isn't a Fortran compiler." Sheesh.


Unfortunately, you have strayed from the point of my post.  If you
look at the oldest text I quoted, I was responding to a question
about the difference between g77 and Compaq Fortran, and to me the
difference is night and day.  If you write standard-conforminig code
like:

module hello
   contains
      subroutine world
         write(*,*) 'Hello, world!'
      end subroutine world
end module hello

program litmus_test
   use hello
   call world
end program litmus_test

the Compaq compiler has no problems with this but my understanding
of g77 is that it has no chance to compile it.  This is not simply
a question of performance but of a vast gulf in capabilities.
Granted, I couched my statement in language that made a semantic
distinction between Fortran compilers as being compilers that at
least make some kind of attempt to keep up with current standards
and Fortran 77 compilers as being compilers that don't (gotcha,
Greg!) and the current audience is one that is not likely to be
sympathetic to my view (if you really wanted to use F90 or F95
you probably wouldn't be running Alpha Linux, would you?).  For
those who prefer or even insist on F90 and are not put off by
Unix, the existence of a Fortran (and by this I mean full F95)
compiler, especially one by Compaq, can make a big difference
in their choice of platform.






