> front of me, but I believe the penalty you are seeing is due to the 
> fact that only 1 predicted index-set target is kept per fetch block
> (16 bytes).  This the fetch blocks with multiple jumps will only
> keep history for one of those jumps, not all of them.
> 
> Keeping 11 bits of index-set prediction data per fetch block already
> eats up a fair bit of space; Keeping 44 bits would probably be extreme 
> overkill.
> 
> At any rate, I'm guessing you'll see the penalty go away not if
> you align the jumps or the targets, but rather if you insure that
> multiple jumps don't exist in the same fetch block.
> 
> Make sense?

It would, if there was more than one jump in the 16-byte block.  Ok,
maybe the chip does not fully decode the instructions for this, but
decoding a frequent instruction like ldah or addq (which is what I
used) as jump would probably seriously hurt branch prediction.

One theory I have is that the performance penalty exists as soon as
the destination register of the instruction before the unaligned
branch target is not $31 (the zero register).  I made a few
experiments, and they seem to support this (but I also found a few
more unexpected variations).

I also made several experiments with the same code in the 16-byte
block, varying the branch target between the first and the second
instruction of the block, and always saw a huge slowdown when the jump
target was not the first instruction.

- anton
