This is pretty common, and can be resolved by forcing the kernel to look at the proper location during boot. The easiest way to do this is to add the following to your lilo.conf:

append="ether=11,0x300,eth0 ether=12,0x320,eth1"

Add this as a single line under your boot image settings. The first parameter is the IRQ, the second is the base memory address, the third is the device name.

Remember to run lilo after modifying lilo.conf, and reboot.

If you like, you can test this by entering the above line at the lilo: prompt at boot time, thusly:

lilo: linux append="ether=11,0x300,eth0 ether=12,0x320,eth1"

This, of course, assumes that your Linux boot section is named linux, and is all on one line...

Hope this helps.

BG


