I don't know if this would be feasible... remember Alpha/NT is 32-bit...
think how dosemu runs 16-bit apps, it requires special hardware support,
called virtual-86 mode IIRC.

Also, Alpha/NT runs under a completely different PALcode than Linux. 
With these problems I don't know how object files could ever be shared
between the two.

The Wine library itself can certainly be ported to Alpha/Linux, and
would aid in porting Win32 applications from source.

The em86 program is horribly slow, unsuprising considering it has to
emulate each x86 instruction.  To make this feasible at all, we would
have to:

 - create some translator, either standalone or just-in-time, to convert
   x86 instructions to native Alpha instructions
 - map library calls to native Alpha libc, libm, X11, etc.

FX!32 already does both.  That's why it performs reasonably well.

donaldlf@cs.rose-hulman.edu wrote:
> -> Will there ever be a native Alpha/Wine to run NT/Alpha apps?

Jeff



