Organization: Deja News - The Leader in Internet Discussion
X-Article-Creation-Date: Mon Apr 05 07:52:34 1999 GMT
Reply-To: donaldlf@cs.rose-hulman.edu
Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.alpha
X-Http-User-Agent: Mozilla/3.01 (X11; I; Linux 2.2.3 alpha)



Hello All,

This is a longish message with a request for an open discussion on
where Linux/AXP is headed. After a long talk last night in a bar
(after Convergence V) some questions were raised that I thought
the users of Linux/AXP should consider.

Assertions for the discussion:
-) The Alpha processor is the best 64 bit CPU currently in production.
-) The Alpha processor has limited support in the world at large.
-) The popularity for CPU is more than likely:
   - Intel
   - Power PC
   - Alpha

Question:
  Where is the Alpha headed and how can we become the number one CPU.

My 5 cents worth.

  I personally think the Alpha is the best CPU in the world however
it suffers from a lack of application support. There are more and more
Unix applications being ported to the 64 bit mode of the Alpha but this
still pales in comparison to the Intel world. I see one of the
biggest advantages that Linux/Intel has is Wine. Why?
  1) The ability to run games. I know this seems childish but lets face
     it most home computers bought are game computers and the home
     market is a large segment of the computer economy.
  2) With Wine the corporate world can leverage there current Windows
     applications until they have Unix varieties.
  3) Some applications (include legacy code) will or is only available
     on the Windows platform.
Now the Alpha has a <HUGE> advantage over every other non Intel platform
that runs Linux. em86
The problem is:
  1) em86 is no longer supported.
  2) em86 does not work with glibc.
  3) em86 can not run Wine.
My current experiences with compiling em86 have been sad. I managed to fix
the compile problems and create a version of em86 that compiles but
due to the fundamental changes in the kernel and libc it no longer works.
I think that is mainly calling arguments but it will take some time to
flush out all of the bugs. The support for Intel/glibc is an even bigger
issue.

The bochs project appears to be working on a em86 like emulator currently.
From the FAQ
>Q: What resources is Bochs Software looking for?
>Bochs Software is looking for financial and equipment support. A back-end for
each CPU out
>there, needs to be written for the dynamic translation being developed
currently. Bochs runs in
>interpreter mode on most every platform already. But, for your platform to run
bochs with
>high-performance dynamic translation mode, I need access to loaner or donor
machines to code the
>CPU specific back-end. Related CPU programming manuals are also desired.
>In addition, I want to adding threading to the CPU emulation and other
components, so multiple
>processor machines will be needed in the near future. I currently have single
processor x86 and
>SPARC machines, though faster versions of those are also greatly appreciated.
>If you are a CPU, OS, or platform vendor, you may benefit greatly by a port of
dynamic
>translation to your product. Your support will lead to much faster development
time, to get bochs
>running in high-performance mode on your product.


I see there are two directions that we in the Linux/AXP community should
consider:
1. Upgrading EM86 to support Wine,glibc and other items.
2. Find hardware to allow bochs to run.

I personally think we should do both. With Wine running users could run single
applications.
Bochs would allow end users to run an entire environment.

The other questions we should consider:
-) Will the AMD-K7 boards support the 21264a chip?
-> Will there ever be a native Alpha/Wine to run NT/Alpha apps?

I realize that most people will say that this entire thread is stupid. To
these people I say simply "maybe so". I went out and bought and Alpha because
I wanted a new computer with 64 math and the challenge of running a different
style of hardware. I also think that the Alpha is the best CPU on the market
today. My greatest fear is that the Alpha will die from lack of support
steaming from the need to reinvent the wheel to get applications to run. I
personally wish every application that I ever need to run ran on an Alpha in
native mode. I just don't see this happening any time soon. I think If we
could upgrade the cross platform support we could attract more people to the
platform for the long term.

I don't actually expect this message to create a public surge in interest. In
fact I will be surprised if anyone even notices it. I just hope it open the
gate to discuss it. I have left off how I would proceeded to this goal for a
reason. I would like to hear (umm read) other people's though on this
subject.

I thank one an all for the bandwidth
Leslie Donaldson

