Christoph Schug wrote:
...
> Well, the same goes for me. I took the latest stuff from
> gatekeeper.dec.com. And the kernel? Hmm. For now I haven't got any kernel
> at all. The only thing I want is this f***** (fine?) MILO prompt. I
> thought MILO is the place where I specify the location of the kernel
> (similar to LILO on the x86 architecture) with the only difference that
> MILO has got some kind of interactive shell (the MILO prompt). I hope I
> got that right.
> 
> The only thing that bothers me is chapter 2 of the MILO HOWTO which says
> that the kernel is part of MILO. Some paragraphs later it is said that the
> kernel is loaded from some file system. So what's right.
> 
...

The ARC BIOS (and the AlphaBIOS if that's not the same) only knows of
DOS FAT
filesystem.  It can boot something from a DOS partition, for instance
ldmilo.exe, MILOs first stage boot loader, which in turn boots MILO. The
kernel
is *not* part of MILO, but some kernel code is part of MILO (i.e. the
interrupt handling, see Alpha Miniloader HOWTO). Since MILO knows about
devices (SCSI, ...) and filesystems (ext2 or whatever) it can load the
kernel directly from any point in the filesystem. That's why you don't
need a 'MILO run' (compared to LILO) when you would like to boot a new
kernel.

That's what the way I think things work. I hope this helps a little bit
:-)
