Jan Geertsma wrote:
> 
> 
> I doubt it, AMD will introduce a slot-1 look-alike named slot-A. To be alpha
> compatible alpha procs should be fitted into a slot-A, don't think alpha
> designers let the market dictate them. Compaq is shipping a lot of their
> computers with AMD, and AMDK7 using the Alpha-bus makes it a good competetor
> to Intel.
> 

Actually IIRC, Slot-A was developed for Alpha by Digital/Compaq,
probably (I can't remember for sure now) with input from AMD. AMD
decided to license the Alpha bus because a) Intel didn't really want to
license theirs to AMD, and b) the Alpha EV-6 bus is a superior bus
anyway. An added benefit (and a design goal) to both AMD and Alpha is
that because there is a common processor interface, motherboards can be
built to accept either processor, allowing better economies of scale for
both markets, and allowing an upgrade (downgrade/crossgrade) path. Also
remember that AMD is still a possible future source of Alpha chips.
Expect to see such mother boards in the not-too-distant future.

/Ryan
(an ex-DEC employee)
Usual disclaimers apply, but all of the above information is in the
public domain somewhere.



