From: anton@mips.complang.tuwien.ac.at (Anton Ertl)
Subject: XP1000 boot disk image available
Date: 1999/04/07
Message-ID: <7ef2rv$duu$1@news.tuwien.ac.at>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 463556529
Organization: Institut fuer Computersprachen, Technische Universitaet Wien
Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.alpha

I have now put an image of the boot disk we use on our XP1000
(including a 2.2.1 kernel) at
http://www.complang.tuwien.ac.at/anton/linux-alpha/xp1000-boot.img
(1440KB).  You can find the modules and config file for this kernel in
http://www.complang.tuwien.ac.at/anton/linux-alpha/config+modules-2.2.1.tar.gz
(400KB).

Write the image to a floppy with dd or rawrite.exe, then put the
floppy into the XP1000, and boot aboot from the SRM console with

boot dva0

Then you can boot the kernel with

vmlinux.gz root=...

Note that the kernel on the floppy was configured to have the IDE
driver as module, so if you want to install a Linux distribution from
the built-in CD-ROM, that's not easy. An install with ftp or NFS
should work, though.

Don't ask me how to do this, we used this boot disk with an
already-installed Linux system.  While we are at it, make absolutely
sure that the floppy was written correctly and your floppy drive works
before reporting problems to me; in my experience boot floppies tend
to be currupted or have read errors a lot of the time.

The boot disk might also work with other Alphas using the SRM console;
the kernel on the floppy does not support all hardware, though (e.g.,
only NCR8xx or Qlogic ISP SCSI host adapter).

- anton
