Sandy Kirby (sandy.kirby@unforgettable.com) wrote:

: I have a Multia (UDB) 166/32MB/2 GB HD and a dead floppy drive.

: Is it possible to boot directly off a CD to install Linux? (I can
: install NT like this (and apparently DEC Unix as well) but I can't
: seem to find any info. on Linux.........)

The following instructions are for the version or ARC firmware that
is installed on my multia running linux. I think it is the newest
version, but you should be able to poke your way through to get the
desired results regardless of version.

The only sucess I have had when booting from cdrom is to boot milo
and then boot linux from milo. You CAN NOT get the whole thing to 
happen from an arc boot entry if you are booting from cdrom. I'll 
have to look at my ide drive boot entry next time I reboot to get 
the actual setup I'm using, as there is an environment you can
set up in milo that will handle the auto booting afterwards, and
that may be what I have running here. The following will get you
started on the linux install directly from cdrom though.

Once the multia boots up into the arc menu, go through the following
actions:

Supplementary menu
Setup the system
Manage boot selection menu
Add a boot selection
New system partition
CDROM
bus 0 scsi id 4 (or whatever your cdrom scsi id is)
osloader directory
\milo\arc\linload.exe
os same partition?
yes
os root directory
(yes, I know that the actual name is longer, but THIS works, udb-noname won't)
\milo\udb-nona
name
cdrom milo
init debugger
no

change a boot selector
cdrom milo
OSLOADOPTIONS
backspace over it to clear the entry and press enter
escape key
escape key
escape key
return key
Boot menu
Boot cdrom milo

After milo boots from cdrom, you will get a MILO> prompt

MILO>boot sr0:/kernels/noname.gz root=/dev/scd0

This will boot linux, and bring you to the redhat install program.
If you go through part of it you will arrive at a point where you
have a prompt on a second console (alt-f2 i think) from where you
can do some fixing up if you need to. Read the redhat docs for how
the installer works, as the environment is a bit weird when it comes
to devices. It makes devices on the fly as it needs them and then 
deletes them on you. 

When you are done fiddling with the ARC firmware setup, you should
have a boot entry with the following data for the cdrom boot.
LOADIDENTIFIER		cdrom milo
SYSTEMPARTITION		scsi(0)cdrom(4)fdisk(0)
OSLOADER		scsi(0)cdrom(4)fdisk(0)\milo\arc\linload.exe
OSLOADPARTITION		scsi(0)cdrom(4)fdisk(0)
OSLOADFILENAME		\milo\udb-nona
OSLOADOPTIONS

You can cross check your work above with that listing.

One thing about ARC and MILO, is that you NEED a small dos fat-12
partition to store the files in to boot from the ide drive or scsi
drive. When setting up using fdisk during the install, make a 5Meg
or so partition to use for this. You will be able to boot from the
hard disk initially using the cdrom milo method and from there use
the utilities that are loaded onto the hard drive to msdos format 
the small partition and put \milo\arc\linload.exe and \milo\udb-noname
onto it using cp. I would rename udb-noname to milo as you won't use 
the versions of milo for other machines. Just put the files into the
root directory of the msdos partition.

I hope this has been detailed enough for people to follow. The setup
for the hard drive afterwards closely parallels the above setup but
uses the proper info for the particular hard drive type and partition
that you use. The least confusing is to use the first partition for
the fat-12 (msdos) boot partition.

